181
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:20,800
looking at bracketing and trending more importantly I can show you what the volatility will look like or the range expansion will look like when we do have a little bit of initiative and then return back to bracketing so looking at the example as you can see from left to right

182
00:21:21,800 --> 00:21:29,700
Initially the market spends in this little box, in this blue box, you can see the market just almost pinging off a lower bound and an upper bound.

183
00:21:29,700 --> 00:21:32,100
Now note, not support, not resistance.

184
00:21:32,100 --> 00:21:40,400
So what we get is we get buyers that respond at lower prices and they then initiate until such a point as they run into responsive sellers.

185
00:21:40,800 --> 00:21:49,500
When they run into those responsive sellers they then back off and we then start to see sellers initiating until they run into again responsive buyers.

186
00:21:49,500 --> 00:21:54,300
So that's what we get initially, a little bit of two-way trade, and we keep pinging between these two points.

187
00:21:54,700 --> 00:22:00,000
Then all of a sudden, you can see three relatively aggressive candles as we move out.

188
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,300
side of the initial bracket.

189
00:22:02,700 --> 00:22:07,300
Now note the first thing to understand is look at the size of the three candles.

190
00:22:07,300 --> 00:22:15,900
Now the reason I like to talk about candles in this respect and I will show you this in profile examples later on but look at the size and the length of those candles.

191
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:22,500
Note how they're the biggest three candles we have in this entire you know since the beginning of this chart.

192
00:22:22,800 --> 00:22:25,400
Now that already shows us initiative.

193
00:22:25,400 --> 00:22:30,600
It shows us that we're seeing a little bit of control factor coming in on the short side.

194
00:22:31,700 --> 00:22:44,300
Again, straight away, one of the clues you need to pick up, when a market moves outside of a bracket, remember what we said, we need excessive force to get the market to move away from what it deems fair value.

195
00:22:44,700 --> 00:22:52,500
And then as you can see, remember, we get the initiative from the sellers until such a point as it runs into responsive buying.

196
00:22:52,800 --> 00:22:59,200
Once it runs into that responsive buying, sellers will then ease off the initiative and prices will start to drift higher.

197
00:22:59,600 --> 00:23:05,000
Note then we move into our second bracketing example where the market just trades between two points.

198
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:16,200
Now what I want to point out is that when we do have initiative moves, and this is a little bit of a clue or a hint as to what we're going to be talking about in strategies later on, but when there is initiative,

199
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:26,200
that initiative point or that break point becomes an extremely important pivot in the market that sellers or that whoever initiated needs to defend.

200
00:23:26,400 --> 00:23:29,900
So as you can see, the first time we retest that break point or that

201
00:23:30,100 --> 00:23:42,100
dotted line you can see how the market you know sort of respects it and we see sort of not just buyers back off the initiation but we see sellers stepping in initiating

202
00:23:42,700 --> 00:23:48,100
fresh shorts again so again we can see the market enters a second sort of bracketing example

203
00:23:49,100 --> 00:23:57,400
Finally, once we take out that key pivot where the seller is initially initiated, note how the market then moves back into the original bracket.

204
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:04,700
Now again, this is not something that's new, you're going to see this very, very often, you know, the market moving in and out of bracketing areas.

205
00:24:05,000 --> 00:24:09,900
The important thing to understand is when are we bracketing and when are we initiating or trending.

206
00:24:10,900 --> 00:24:15,600
So again, we then have our third box, another example of bracketing between buyers and sellers.

207
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:26,800
Again, no tell when we saw the initiation in the first box, that key level where that dotted line is now acts again as an area where we have responsive buying.

208
00:24:26,800 --> 00:24:34,400
So what was initially sort of almost call it support then became resistance and has now become support again.

209
00:24:34,700 --> 00:24:45,000
And then again, no tell once we take out that area, we get initiative again from the sellers until they run into responsive buyers, etc., etc., etc.

210
00:24:45,200 --> 00:24:49,100
So this continues to occur over a period of time.

211
00:24:49,100 --> 00:24:52,800
So what I want to point out and illustrate to you is that.

212
00:24:53,200 --> 00:24:56,700
Trade location, and we're going to talk a lot about trade location in the coming slides.

213
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,300
Trade location defines our risk.

214
00:24:59,400 --> 00:24:59,800
In other words,

215
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:14,100
What I mean by that, if we are looking for buy side trades in that first box for instance, in the first bracketing, if we're looking for buy side trades towards the high momentum trades, we're taking on a lot more risk.

216
00:25:14,700 --> 00:25:19,200
Equally, if we're looking for sell side trades towards the bottom of it, we are taking on high risk.

217
00:25:19,400 --> 00:25:32,200
However, once we break and show the force behind the selling and the initiative, then there's a high probability opportunity to look for momentum plays or trending type plays.

218
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:34,100
So just note the crucial difference there.

219
00:25:34,300 --> 00:25:48,000
It's okay to interpret a market being sideways and having a potential to break, but it's imperative that you don't preempt the break, but rather wait for those clues, wait for those little signals, and then go with the momentum.

220
00:25:49,800 --> 00:25:57,500
Now we're going to get to the point where we talk about how do we identify when a market moves from trending back into bracketing.

221
00:25:57,600 --> 00:26:06,600
I'm going to show you three nice little setups, strategies, techniques you can use to observe and identify potential turning points.

222
00:26:06,800 --> 00:26:11,000
We will develop these strategies also further on the blackboard later on.

223
00:26:11,000 --> 00:26:13,600
So let's go through them one by one.

224
00:26:13,600 --> 00:26:22,400
So there are two methods, but three examples that we're going to talk about when we talk about potential turning points from a trend back into bracketing.

225
00:26:22,700 --> 00:26:27,100
Now, the first one is the less frequent one, which is a loss of momentum.

226
00:26:27,100 --> 00:26:37,200
So almost like, you know, we have a flurry of force, some very strong initiative buying, and all of a sudden, you know, prices are moving higher and higher and advertising higher and higher prices.

227
00:26:37,500 --> 00:26:42,700
All of a sudden, there's just a real lack of interest to show initiative at higher prices.

228
00:26:42,800 --> 00:26:48,600
So it's not to say that buyers don't want to be long, it's just that they don't want to show initiative at higher prices.

229
00:26:48,600 --> 00:26:54,500
Now, at this point, volume simply dries up or the lack of interest in new business dries up.

230
00:26:54,500 --> 00:27:00,000
Now, we need that new business in order to continue to see the market initiating at higher prices.

231
00:27:00,200 --> 00:27:05,800
So, if everyone is fully committed and no one is left to participate, well, then it's very simple.

232
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:10,400
There's a lot of, you know, you'll have obviously the initial longs that bought at the beginning of the trend.

233
00:27:10,600 --> 00:27:13,400
You'll have more longs that bought sort of in the mature phase.

234
00:27:13,600 --> 00:27:17,100
And then you'll have some weaker longs that bought sort of at the back end of the trend.

235
00:27:17,300 --> 00:27:22,400
And those weaker longs are likely to be the first ones that will liquidate those longs.

236
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:24,400
And then we ultimately start bracketing.

237
00:27:24,500 --> 00:27:25,200
So.

238
00:27:26,200 --> 00:27:29,700
This is the first sort of example that we will talk about.

239
00:27:30,200 --> 00:27:32,900
when we talk about potential reversals in the market.

240
00:27:32,900 --> 00:27:37,400
And I'm going to show you examples and how they look in the volume profiles in the slides ahead.

241
00:27:37,800 --> 00:27:40,600
The next example is what we call an excess top.

242
00:27:41,100 --> 00:27:43,700
Again, this is probably the most frequent kind of top.

243
00:27:44,300 --> 00:27:50,300
As I would have spoken to you about in Module 1, this is the kind of top where we see a tail in the market, or we almost see

244
00:27:50,700 --> 00:28:01,200
the market goes from initiative, it runs into response of other time frame players, and then quickly backs off as prices are being advertised at either a discount or a premium.

245
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:06,100
So the market makes a dramatic move higher or lower, so that's the initiative.

246
00:28:06,300 --> 00:28:13,100
Again, this can be on, I don't like to say lower volume necessarily, but relatively okay volume.

247
00:28:13,100 --> 00:28:15,100
So nothing flash, nothing amazing.

248
00:28:15,800 --> 00:28:20,000
And what we then quite, and then what you then see is quite a quick reversal.

249
00:28:20,200 --> 00:28:25,000
And the setup I'm going to show you is called a V reversal or an inverted V reversal.

250
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:31,300
And so effectively, we get quite a strong auction reversal process.

251
00:28:31,500 --> 00:28:38,700
And this can be done it doesn't have to be on low volume it can be on high volume but we tend to see this done on relatively

252
00:28:39,300 --> 00:28:51,300
sort of average kind of volume so this is our second kind of you know top which we talk about now a more sort of almost dramatic and exciting sort of excess top would be our third example

253
00:28:52,900 --> 00:28:57,100
So just note an excessive reversal can appear to be on high volume.

254
00:28:57,200 --> 00:29:00,000
So it can look as though something crazy is going on in the market.

255
00:29:00,200 --> 00:29:03,900
and there's a lot of aggressiveness and there's almost aggressive trend.

256
00:29:04,800 --> 00:29:08,200
But the important thing to understand is the high volume isn't on the excess.

257
00:29:08,200 --> 00:29:18,700
In other words, it isn't from the buyers buying up and initiating at higher prices, but it's actually on these higher prices bringing in other responsive sellers.

258
00:29:19,100 --> 00:29:23,300
And again, vice versa, obviously it could be to the downside as well.

259
00:29:23,400 --> 00:29:31,900
So it's those higher prices being advertised that bring response from the other time frame and that is what creates potential for high volume.

260
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:37,000
But note again, this doesn't have to be on high volume, it can be on low or higher volume.

261
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:48,600
Now, the more excessive and aggressive version of this excess top is what we call a strong excess reversal.

262
00:29:48,800 --> 00:29:52,500
And how we identify between the two is very simple.

263
00:29:52,800 --> 00:29:59,000
When we get a strong excess reversal, we tend to see a pickup in the volatility, so range expansion.

264
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:03,300
We see quite a lot of volume and we see excessive prices.

265
00:30:03,400 --> 00:30:07,300
So we see a combination of volatility, volume and excessive prices.

266
00:30:07,300 --> 00:30:15,900
Whereas in just an excess top, we tend to see a pickup in relative volume and obviously a reversal.

267
00:30:15,900 --> 00:30:21,000
So the big difference here is we see the volatility and the volume change quite dramatically.

268
00:30:22,300 --> 00:30:29,600
The reason we want to identify and differentiate between these two types is that when we have these real sort of excessive type

269
00:30:30,100 --> 00:30:41,900
you know tops being formed you know we get very strong reversals but more important the tops or bottoms they make become crucially important pivot points and that's why we identify between the two because

270
00:30:42,700 --> 00:30:52,500
a top that's made on on volatile volume and excessive prices is far likely to hold for a longer period of time and it does usually signal

271
00:30:53,100 --> 00:30:59,400
almost like the end of a trending market so that's why we want to differentiate between the two

272
00:31:00,700 --> 00:31:11,500
Now, what we can say, which is important, and this is a phrase that quite a few sort of professionals like to use, is that highs and lows in market, in other words, longer term highs and lows.

273
00:31:11,500 --> 00:31:14,500
And again, I use the word longer term in a relative manner.

274
00:31:14,900 --> 00:31:17,600
You could be a day trader and it could be the high for the day.

275
00:31:17,800 --> 00:31:21,100
You could be a weekly trader or a monthly trader and this could be that high.

276
00:31:21,100 --> 00:31:25,500
So highs and lows tend to be made on increased volatility.

277
00:31:25,500 --> 00:31:37,600
So what I'm trying to point out is that when we see this extreme excess tops and bottoms, they tend to be made on volatility, volume, and very, very excess prices.

278
00:31:37,800 --> 00:31:40,300
And they tend to hold for a long, long period of time.

279
00:31:40,400 --> 00:31:45,500
And that's why we definitely identify between the two types of excessive tops.

280
00:31:45,700 --> 00:31:48,500
So let's now go and have a look at what these examples are.

281
00:31:48,600 --> 00:31:53,400
I'm going to show you how all three of them look, and then hopefully you can develop a better understanding.

282
00:31:54,600 --> 00:32:00,000
So our first example, as you'll recall, is the one where we just simply lose momentum.

283
00:32:00,700 --> 00:32:04,100
This is the less frequent kind of top you're going to see.

284
00:32:04,200 --> 00:32:11,300
So as you can see in the chart or in the volume profile, we have a lot of TPO overlap at this high.

285
00:32:11,300 --> 00:32:20,200
So you can see almost the S, the T, the U, the V and the W print almost all sort of sitting at these prices, these higher prices being advertised.

286
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:24,000
But there's a real lack of interest to initiate another move higher.

287
00:32:24,000 --> 00:32:30,200
So it's almost like buyers and sellers are saying, well, we're happily accepting that the market is now trading these prices.

288
00:32:30,200 --> 00:32:39,000
So again, we can see that this is just almost like a slow, you know, just a slowing type of momentum that's occurring at the high of the day.

289
00:32:40,400 --> 00:32:51,300
Some of the questions we need to ask ourselves when we talk about these three different tops being formed is firstly, remember, when a market is moving either up or down, I like to have a phrase

290
00:32:52,100 --> 00:32:56,300
and just remind myself that it takes time for a big ship to turn around.

291
00:32:56,300 --> 00:32:59,400
So remember, the market equally is a big ship.

292
00:32:59,500 --> 00:33:03,500
So it does take time to turn around a lot of the time.

293
00:33:03,500 --> 00:33:09,400
And knowing this again will keep you from maybe fading very aggressive trends too soon.

294
00:33:09,600 --> 00:33:19,000
But also, it'll make you just be that little bit patient to wait for some form of objectivity that the volume profile can potentially show you.

295
00:33:19,200 --> 00:33:23,300
What you can see in this example is how we can see that real loss of momentum.

296
00:33:23,500 --> 00:33:30,000
We got the initiative early on from buyers and then there was just a real lack of interest and a real lack of new business.

297
00:33:30,200 --> 00:33:31,200
at the higher prices.

298
00:33:31,200 --> 00:33:37,100
So that was sort of our clue that a potential loss of momentum was occurring.

299
00:33:37,200 --> 00:33:50,200
Now remember when we get this loss of momentum type top it's not a particularly strong top it's merely just a short-term turning point where buyers don't want to initiate higher just yet

300
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:54,100
and so at that point we start to get a little more two-way trade.

301
00:33:55,000 --> 00:34:04,100
Now, some of the questions we need to ask ourselves when talking and thinking about these kinds of tops is, you know, what kind of volume is being traded at the extreme?

302
00:34:04,100 --> 00:34:11,700
And what you'll tend to find is that there is not aggressive kind of volume, but a lot of two-way trade between buyers and sellers.

303
00:34:11,700 --> 00:34:16,200
So, you know, we will see a lot of trade being facilitated at the highs and lows.

304
00:34:16,200 --> 00:34:25,900
And remember what we said, you know, in a very strong auction process, we don't want to see a lot of facilitation at what we deem premium prices or discount prices.

305
00:34:26,200 --> 00:34:35,500
When we see this, you know, a high amount of volume being facilitated is telling us there's quite a lot of acceptance, you know, at that price point.

306
00:34:35,700 --> 00:34:40,700
And remember, highs and lows are not made on acceptance.

307
00:34:40,800 --> 00:34:47,900
Rather, they're made on sort of initiative being taken from sellers at higher prices as prices are advertised at a premium.

308
00:34:48,100 --> 00:34:53,000
And likewise for buyers when prices are being priced at a discount.

309
00:34:54,000 --> 00:34:56,800
Then, what kind of auction is occurring at the extreme?

310
00:34:56,800 --> 00:34:59,900
So again, note, at very aggressive tops and bottoms,

311
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:02,900
we tend to see a strong auction process.

312
00:35:03,200 --> 00:35:07,800
Sellers take advantage of premium prices and buyers don't buy at premium prices.

313
00:35:07,800 --> 00:35:13,800
So again, in this example, we can see that we see a lot of two-way trade between the buyers and sellers.

314
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:20,200
So again, whilst the market did back away off these higher prices, it is telling us about the kind of top

315
00:35:20,600 --> 00:35:31,900
that is in the market and more importantly, the likelihood or the probability that at some point in the future, we'll either retest or take out that high and new buyers then continue to move prices higher.

316
00:35:32,900 --> 00:35:36,000
And lastly, the rotation and the speed on the way down.

317
00:35:36,000 --> 00:35:37,000
Remember what I said to you.

318
00:35:37,400 --> 00:35:49,800
As these buyers run into responsive sellers at higher prices, if those responsive sellers turn and become initiative sellers, the market will very quickly reverse what it did on the upside.

319
00:35:50,000 --> 00:36:04,400
So the mere fact that not only did we have almost a slowish type reversal, but again a retest, is very suggestive that this market, whilst there was a little bit of initiative

320
00:36:04,800 --> 00:36:13,000
taken from the sellers, we do want to assess how the buyers and sellers are interacting at various prices.

321
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:17,500
When there's an aggressive interaction at higher prices, that is telling us one thing.

322
00:36:17,700 --> 00:36:21,200
When there's not such an aggressive interaction, that's telling us another thing.

323
00:36:21,500 --> 00:36:29,500
What's imperative to understand is this kind of interaction because that will determine what prices are likely to do going forward.

324
00:36:30,500 --> 00:36:34,600
Let's now look at the second example, which is an excess top.

325
00:36:35,900 --> 00:36:41,900
Now, straight away, if you look at the picture without me even bringing down the text, you can see how the market,

326
00:36:42,400 --> 00:36:50,000
once we sort of ran into a price point where prices were being advertised, almost at an extreme or an excess point where it was

327
00:36:50,200 --> 00:37:00,100
a very, very good premium price, we saw how quickly the sellers interacted and they very quickly shut off the buying and took the prices back lower down.

328
00:37:00,200 --> 00:37:09,000
So what we want to gain, you know, sort of remember is that excess tops can be made on relatively low volume or high volume.

329
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:10,500
There's no hard, fast rule.

330
00:37:10,800 --> 00:37:18,000
The important thing to understand is the auctioning that occurs at the highs or the lows when we form an excess top.

331
00:37:18,100 --> 00:37:22,100
Now, what you traditionally see on the volume profile is some form of a tail.

332
00:37:22,500 --> 00:37:32,200
Again, the length of the tail, depending on the market you're trading, is crucial because that is telling us how quickly the other time frame was shut back down.

333
00:37:32,300 --> 00:37:38,300
So in this example, we can see not the biggest tail in the world, but a relatively sort of bigger tail.

334
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:45,700
And that's telling us that sellers very quickly shut down the buyers that we're initiating and move prices quite quickly lower.

335
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:58,900
Note as well how once we auctioned that top, once we found that top, the market then very quickly navigated away from that top and sellers took complete control and continued to initiate prices.

336
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:00,000
And we even took...

337
00:38:00,100 --> 00:38:03,600
out the low of the day and extend it towards the downside.

338
00:38:03,700 --> 00:38:10,400
So we can see we saw some real strong initiative being taken by the sellers and the buyers got overwhelmed in this example.

339
00:38:10,400 --> 00:38:23,300
So again we can see how these X's top not only do they give us you know the tail gave us an early clue but it gave us quite a bit of opportunity to hop on with the initiative selling that continued to occur over the course of the day.

340
00:38:24,800 --> 00:38:26,800
Then note the volume in the extreme.

341
00:38:27,300 --> 00:38:32,700
We could see where in the first example with a loss of momentum we saw a lot of two-way trade.

342
00:38:32,900 --> 00:38:42,000
In this example, in the top quartile, in the higher prices, we would have seen a real lack of volume being facilitated between buyers and sellers.

343
00:38:42,300 --> 00:38:42,600
Why?

344
00:38:42,600 --> 00:38:45,100
Because the buyers got shut down very quickly.

345
00:38:45,100 --> 00:38:51,800
So there was a lack of interest from buyers to be buying at higher prices and sellers quickly took advantage of those higher prices.

346
00:38:52,100 --> 00:38:57,200
Thus, we wouldn't have had as much two-way trade as we would have had in the first example used.

347
00:38:57,600 --> 00:38:59,900
And then the rotation of the speed on the way down.

348
00:39:00,100 --> 00:39:06,300
Like I said in the earlier slide, it's all about assessing how does the market move back down.

349
00:39:06,300 --> 00:39:13,400
Is it aggressive, in which case we're seeing aggressive initiative, or is it a lot more slow where we're seeing a lot more two-way interaction?

350
00:39:13,800 --> 00:39:21,800
Let's go to the third example and talk about the aggressive X's top and see how that one looks on a chart.

351
00:39:23,300 --> 00:39:30,000
As you'll see, just looking at the chart again, very, very similar to the second example, except this time around,

352
00:39:30,400 --> 00:39:38,400
The two major differences are, note, the size of the tail, so the size and the speed of the rejection, but also then the rotation down.

353
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:48,300
What we saw in the second example was there was still quite a bit of, as we were moving down, there was two-way trade being facilitated as buyers were responding as prices moved lower.

354
00:39:48,600 --> 00:40:00,200
In this example, however, note how we see very little buying response until the market almost rushes and aggressively sells off and rushes back into response of buying.

355
00:40:00,200 --> 00:40:03,600
So crucial to identify the two major differences.

356
00:40:03,700 --> 00:40:13,500
One, the excessive initiative taken from sellers at the higher prices, but then also the aggressive initiative taken even as prices continued to move lower.

357
00:40:13,500 --> 00:40:18,100
So there was a lack of response from buyers and that's what created the aggressive move down.

358
00:40:19,200 --> 00:40:22,300
So note, remember we said the aggressive excess is top.

359
00:40:22,500 --> 00:40:26,700
It includes three things, volume, volatility, and a loss of momentum.

360
00:40:26,700 --> 00:40:29,600
So in terms of volatility, what do we mean by that?

361
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:36,700
If you look at the size of the two candles or the three candles before we formed the Y

362
00:40:37,600 --> 00:40:47,000
tail top, you can see that there's quite a bit of overlap between prices, a lot of inside type trade, a lot of two-way trade between buyers and sellers.

363
00:40:47,200 --> 00:40:57,600
And then all of a sudden, we get this Y tail being formed, but it takes out, you know, the Y tail is the largest, you know, sort of TPO candle on the entire day.

364
00:40:57,600 --> 00:40:59,900
So it has the biggest range of the entire day.

